[1mAMP Protocol Translation (C → Rust)[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The AMP (Abstract Message Protocol) is a simple binary message format with:[0m[0m
- A 1-byte header: upper nibble = version, lower nibble = argument count[0m[0m
- For each argument: 4-byte big-endian length prefix followed by the data[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Three functions were implemented:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. [38;5;10mamp_encode(argv)[0m — Builds the binary message: writes the header byte, then for each argument writes its length as 4 big-endian bytes followed by the raw data. Since the Rust API uses [38;5;10mString[0m, raw bytes are mapped to chars via latin1 encoding ([38;5;10mb as char[0m).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
2. [38;5;10mAmp::decode(buf)[0m — Parses the header byte to extract version and argc, then stores the remainder of the buffer (after the header) in [38;5;10mself.buf[0m for subsequent argument decoding.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
3. [38;5;10mAmp::decode_arg()[0m — Reads the 4-byte length prefix, extracts the argument. The tricky part: it returns [38;5;10m&str[0m borrowing from [38;5;10mself[0m while also needing to advance a cursor. Since no fields can be added to the struct, the solution rearranges [38;5;10mself.buf[0m to put remaining data first and the decoded argument at the end, then returns a slice of the tail. This allows the next call to read the next argument's length prefix from the front.[0m[0m
